    "created_at": "2021-09-16T09:48:50Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-09-16T09:48:50Z",
    "body": "> So if I'm reading this correctly, the effective change here is to ignore `<meta charset>` inside `<noscript>` always (including XHR?). Is that right?\r\n\r\nNot yet clear if in XHR, but at least in normal loads in a browsing context, yes.\r\n\r\n> Do you have a sense for compat here?\r\n\r\nOnly conjecture that putting `<meta charset>` in `<noscript>` isn't something that authors like to do. No data. (Currently, `<meta charset>` in `<noscript>` is non-conforming for authoring, but that doesn't mean anything for compat.)\r\n\r\n(Here's different `noscript` test case that demonstrates that Gecko and IE only see the `meta` using a spec-like prescanner while WebKit and Blink see it with the real tokenizer (in prescan): https://hsivonen.com/test/moz/meta/in-noscript-ncr.htm )\r\n\r\n----\r\n\r\nI think we need to reconsider the authoring conformance requirements here.\r\n\r\nThe current requirements for the charset meta are:\r\n\r\n* Must be within the first 1024 bytes.\r\n* Must not use character references.\r\n* Must be a child of the head element.\r\n* Must declare UTF-8.\r\n\r\nWith the change proposed here, the requirement not to use character references is unnecessary and also difficult to check for.\r\n\r\nWhat should be done about the 1024-byte requirement? In general, it's not great for a UA of any kind to have to wait for many bytes. OTOH, if the page declares the same encoding as its same-origin parent, if there is one, or UTF-8 otherwise, with the new Firefox code that I'm working on, it's not actually _that_ bad for the encoding meta to appear later. It does delay DOM building and layout still, though.\r\n\r\nFirst child of head would be better advice than 1024 bytes, but it would also make many pages that are OK in practice to fail the requirement.\r\n\r\nWith the new Firefox code that I'm working on, it is slightly inefficient for there to be non-ASCII characters before the encoding meta if the meta ends up declaring an encoding other than the same as its same-origin parent's or UTF-8 otherwise. However, if both the page and its same-origin parent comply with the requirement to use UTF-8, this point doesn't need a separate conformance requirement to optimize.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
