    "created_at": "2021-09-30T06:35:48Z",
    "updated_at": "2021-09-30T06:35:48Z",
    "body": "The foreign content CDATA issue is probably even less significant for Web compat than the `noscript` issue. However, it's a bigger deal in terms of what magnitude of implementation effort asked from which engine(s).\r\n\r\nWhen I filed this issue, I thought changing the `noscript` behavior would have been a tiny patch for WebKit and Blink. However, the foreign content CDATA behavior would be a more complex change to the WebKit/Blink meta scan token handler: To get real tree builder-equivalent behavior, more complex foreign content state management is needed (at least another level of listing special element names). OTOH, in the Gecko case, meta scanning CDATA sections as bogus comments couldn't be even approximated by a nested parser like `noscript`. Instead, the parsing would need to diverge into a known-to-fail speculation in a special mode that would emulate ignorance of CDATA sections.\r\n\r\nOn both `noscript` and CDATA points, I expect defining meta discovery in terms of the real tree builder to be better than defining the WebKit/Blink special non-tree-builder token handler for any user agent that wishes to bet that most documents are going to declare UTF-8 and don't want to rewind the stream or retokenize in that case. Even though speccing the exact thing that Blink and WebKit now do would be the least effort for Blink and WebKit now, how do Blink and WebKit developers feel about keeping the door open for that kind of UTF-8 bet?\r\n\r\nOther ideas?\r\n\r\nIn any case, I hope that it's considered OK to land WPTs that test the overlap of this proposal and the WebKit/Blink behavior even before whatever spec change we end up making here lands? (I.e. I'm hoping it's OK to land https://hsivonen.com/test/moz/meta/in-svg-in-cdata.htm in WPT if I don't land https://hsivonen.com/test/moz/meta/in-svg-in-cdata-after-gt.htm in WPT.)",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
